   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Alright, what nonsense is this?\n",
    "\n",
    "Alright, gather round. Somebody - not naming names, but it was a past student, and no, it wasn't me, I'm a *professional* - wrote random custom code for a fraud model instead of just importing the one that already works. Bold move. Very \"I don't need a life jacket, I've seen the ocean before.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "You're going to run this thing and it's going to hand you a block of metrics that look printable, screenshot-able, LinkedIn-post-able. The model is going to feel *confident*. The numbers are going to feel *load-bearing*.\n",
    "\n",
    "They are not load-bearing. \n",
    "\n",
    "That's not a model readout, that's garbage that learned to wear a lab coat and introduce itself with a very confident title. Let's go find out what it's actually hiding under there.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b565fd5d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Run It\n",
    "\n",
    "Restart the kernel, run every cell top to bottom, and go look at the printed metrics block at the bottom before you read one more word of my beautiful commentary.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b7a9137b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The Math Clue (pay attention, this is the whole case)\n",
    "\n",
    "Your clue is this confusion matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "|                | Predicted Fraud | Predicted Legit |\n",
    "|----------------|:---------------:|:----------------:|\n",
    "| **Actually Fraud** | True Positive (TP)  | False Negative (FN) |\n",
    "| **Actually Legit** | False Positive (FP) | True Negative (TN)  |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2deb2765",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Your Mission\n",
    "\n",
    "Somewhere in this notebook, a number is answering the wrong question with total confidence. Go read it like you don't trust it because you shouldn't. Figure out where it went off the rails. I'm not telling you the line number. Consider it a character-building exercise.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5f64579f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Submission\n",
    "\n",
    "Fork this repo → fix the bug → open a PR → wait for a human to confirm the fix is correct and the symptom is resolved → receive your title.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "db48b498",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Load and Inspect the Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "15f0b021",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load the raw transaction data\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(\"data/creditcard.csv\")\n",
    "print(df.shape)\n",
    "print(df[\"Class\"].value_counts(normalize=True))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5394a07e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "*Two hundred eighty-four thousand rows and the thing we actually care about (fraud) is a rounding error in the pie chart. This is normal. This is fine. This is also exactly the kind of dataset where a mislabeled metric goes completely unnoticed for months, because \"it looks pretty good\" is a very low bar when almost everything is class 0 anyway.*\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c64f94bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Preprocess and Split"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4afc8f59",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f9d51be1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Train a Baseline Classifier"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Scale Time and Amount AFTER split to avoid data leakage\n",
        "feature_cols_raw = [c for c in df.columns if c.startswith(\"V\")] + [\"Amount\", \"Time\"]\n",
        "X = df[feature_cols_raw]\n",
        "y = df[\"Class\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=42, stratify=y\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "scaler_amount = StandardScaler()\n",
        "scaler_time = StandardScaler()\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train = X_train.copy()\n",
        "X_test = X_test.copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train[\"Amount_scaled\"] = scaler_amount.fit_transform(X_train[[\"Amount\"]])\n",
        "X_train[\"Time_scaled\"] = scaler_time.fit_transform(X_train[[\"Time\"]])\n",
        "\n",
        "X_test[\"Amount_scaled\"] = scaler_amount.transform(X_test[[\"Amount\"]])\n",
        "X_test[\"Time_scaled\"] = scaler_time.transform(X_test[[\"Time\"]])\n",
        "\n",
        "final_cols = [c for c in X_train.columns if c.startswith(\"V\")] + [\"Amount_scaled\", \"Time_scaled\"]\n",
        "X_train = X_train[final_cols]\n",
        "X_test = X_test[final_cols]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(X_train.shape, X_test.shape)\n"
      ]
   "id": "92cf5fa5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Train a baseline logistic regression with class weighting to\n",
    "# account for the severe class imbalance\n",
    "clf = LogisticRegression(class_weight=\"balanced\", max_iter=1000, random_state=42)\n",
    "clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "\n",
    "y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n",
    "y_proba = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"ROC-AUC:\", roc_auc_score(y_test, y_proba))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "221ddb84",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "*ROC-AUC doesn't touch precision or recall at all - it's just here as a sanity check that the model itself isn't garbage. Keep that number in your pocket. Might be useful. Might not. I'm not your GPS.*\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "33e0adae",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Evaluation Metrics"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ff52fd23",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute precision, recall, and F1 from the confusion matrix components,\n",
    "# since we want full control over the counting logic rather than relying\n",
    "# on a library implementation\n",
    "def evaluate_custom_metrics(y_true, y_pred):\n",
    "    y_true = np.asarray(y_true)\n",
    "    y_pred = np.asarray(y_pred)\n",
    "\n",
    "    tp = np.sum((y_true == 1) & (y_pred == 1))\n",
    "    fp = np.sum((y_true == 0) & (y_pred == 1))\n",
    "    fn = np.sum((y_true == 1) & (y_pred == 0))\n",
    "    tn = np.sum((y_true == 0) & (y_pred == 0))\n",
    "\n",
    "    precision = tp / (tp + fp)\n",
    "    recall = tp / (tp + fn)\n",
    "    f1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)\n",
    "\n",
    "    return {\n",
    "        \"tp\": int(tp), \"fp\": int(fp), \"fn\": int(fn), \"tn\": int(tn),\n",
    "        \"precision\": precision, \"recall\": recall, \"f1\": f1,\n",
    "    }\n",
    "\n",
    "metrics = evaluate_custom_metrics(y_test, y_pred)\n",
    "for k, v in metrics.items():\n",
    "    print(f\"{k}: {v}\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dcc551d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Report the Results"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9b61c04f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Summarize the model's fraud-catching performance for the writeup\n",
    "print(f\"Precision: {metrics['precision']:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Recall:    {metrics['recall']:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"F1:        {metrics['f1']:.3f}\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "569805fb",
